        <div id="why-ceiling-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Ceiling Paint Different From Wall Paint?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Ceiling paint is tuned for overhead work in three ways, thickness, sheen and spatter, and often in a fourth, open time. It is not simply white wall paint sold in a different can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thicker, so it stays on the roller</h3>

          <p>
            A thicker, more viscous paint holds on the roller instead of dripping, and it is less likely to fling fine droplets off as the roller spins. That alone makes overhead work far less messy and much kinder to the floors and furniture underneath.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flatter, so light does not catch it</h3>

          <p>
            Ceiling paints are nearly always flat or ultra-flat. Windows and lamps light a ceiling at a low angle, which exaggerates any shine, so even a little sheen reveals roller laps, patches and seams. Flat paint scatters light evenly and hides them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More open time for a wet edge</h3>

          <p>
            Many ceiling paints also stay workable longer. Open time is the window in which wet paint can still blend with the next section without leaving a visible lap. Overhead work is slower and clumsier than walls, so those extra minutes make it easier to keep a wet edge across a big ceiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you swap wall and ceiling paint?</h3>

          <p>
            Wall paint in a flat finish will go on a ceiling, but you give up spatter control and some hiding power. Ceiling paint on walls is fine in low-traffic rooms, yet flat ceiling paint is not made to be scrubbed and marks easily. Each is designed for its own job, and ceiling paint costs little more than wall paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to look for on the can</h3>

          <p>
            In the store, read the label language. Terms such as spatter-resistant, ultra-flat or dead-flat, and extended open time are good signs, along with a coverage figure and recoat time on the data sheet. A can that says only interior flat white, with no ceiling-specific claims, is usually a general-purpose flat paint: it will work, but it helps you less overhead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sheen-light" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Sheen Should Ceiling Paint Be?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flat or ultra-flat for almost every ceiling, with a wipeable matte, eggshell or satin kept for bathrooms, kitchens and laundry rooms. The reason comes down to how light hits a ceiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why side light exposes flaws</h3>

          <p>
            Daylight from a window, or a lamp near a wall, strikes the ceiling at a shallow angle. At that angle every bump, roller ridge and change in sheen throws a shadow or reflects differently. A patch you cannot see looking straight up can stand out from across the room in late-afternoon light.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How flat paint hides them</h3>

          <p>
            Flat and ultra-flat paints blunt that effect. Ultra-flat ceiling paints carry very little sheen, so the whole ceiling reflects light the same way, even where the film is a little thicker or a patch soaked it up differently. That is why premium ceiling paints earn their price in rooms with big windows, skylights or wall-mounted lights.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a little sheen is worth it</h3>

          <p>
            Bathrooms, kitchens and laundry rooms get steam, grease and moisture, and a flat finish there can stain or grow mildew. In those rooms a mildew-resistant paint in matte, eggshell or satin is often the better choice: you accept slightly more visible flaws in return for a surface you can wipe clean.
          </p>

