        <div id="color" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Should a Ceiling Be White or a Color?
          </h2>

          <p>
            White suits most ceilings because it bounces the most light back into the room, but a softly tinted white or a deliberate color can work well if you test it overhead first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why ceiling white is the default</h3>

          <p>
            White ceilings make rooms feel taller and brighter. Ceiling whites are usually formulated with a high light reflectance value, a measure of how much light a color reflects, and untinted ceiling white sits near the top of that scale.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tinting the white to suit the walls</h3>

          <p>
            Beside warm wall colors, pure bright white can look stark or faintly blue. Many designers tint the ceiling a touch, either toward a warmer white or toward a heavily diluted version of the wall color. Ask the paint store to tint ceiling paint at a quarter or half strength of the wall color, and the ceiling will relate to the walls without darkening the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Going darker on purpose</h3>

          <p>
            A colored ceiling is a design decision. Darker ceilings make a room feel lower and cozier, which can suit a large bedroom, dining room or den with tall ceilings. If you go dark, use a paint meant for that color, because heavily tinting a white ceiling base can leave poor coverage. Premium wall paints in flat or matte finishes are often used for colored ceilings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test samples overhead, morning and evening</h3>

          <p>
            Paint a sample board and hold it up in the room in both morning and evening light. Ceilings sit in shadow most of the time, so colors look darker overhead than on a store chip, and a shade that looks right on a wall may read a step darker above you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceilings borrow color from the room</h3>

          <p>
            Strong wall colors, wood floors and even a green lawn outside a window bounce tinted light upward, so a white ceiling in a room with deep blue walls will look faintly blue. That is one more reason to judge samples in place rather than white chips under store lighting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stains-prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Prep a Ceiling Before Painting?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seal stains, repair cracks and nail pops, clean off grease and soap, and test old textured ceilings before you disturb them. The paint goes on last.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seal stains with a stain-blocking primer</h3>

          <p>
            Ceiling paint alone will not hide water stains, smoke damage, nicotine or marker; they bleed through water-based paint, sometimes within hours. Seal them first with a stain-blocking primer such as a shellac-based or oil-based product, then apply the ceiling paint. Before you seal a water stain, make sure the leak is fixed and the drywall is dry and sound. Solvent-based primers give off far stronger fumes than latex paint, so open windows, run a fan blowing out and follow the ventilation directions on the can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Repair cracks and nail pops</h3>

          <p>
            Fill hairline cracks with lightweight spackle; cracks that keep coming back at drywall joints may need retaping. Reset nail pops or swap them for drywall screws, then cover with compound. Sand the patches smooth and spot-prime them so they do not flash through the finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean off grease and soap</h3>

          <p>
            Grease and soap residue stop paint from bonding, so clean the ceiling first, above all in kitchens and bathrooms. Wipe it with a mild degreasing cleaner, rinse with clean water and let it dry. In a bathroom, treat any mildew with a suitable cleaner, rinse and dry thoroughly, and check that the exhaust fan works before you paint. Our <a href="/best-mold-remover/">mold remover guide</a> covers which cleaner suits which surface.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">In an older home, get the texture and paint tested before you scrape or sand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Popcorn and acoustic textures applied before the 1980s may contain asbestos, and paint in homes built before 1978 may contain lead. Send a sample for testing before you scrape, sand or remove any texture. Painting over intact texture is usually safer than disturbing it.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Handle textured ceilings gently</h3>

          <p>
            Popcorn ceilings soak up paint and can loosen when wet, so use a thick-nap roller with light pressure, or spray with a <a href="/best-paint-sprayer/">paint sprayer</a> to avoid pulling texture off. Knockdown and orange-peel textures roll more easily, but they still want a thicker nap than a smooth ceiling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Tools Do You Need to Paint a Ceiling?
          </h2>

          <p>
            An extension pole and a sturdy roller frame, a quality cover with the right nap, an angled sash brush, a stepladder and drop cloths. Getting these right saves more trouble than switching paints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An extension pole and a solid frame</h3>

          <p>
            Rolling from the floor on a pole keeps your arms lower, which cuts fatigue and improves control. A pole that extends from about 2 to 4 feet suits standard 8 to 9 foot ceilings, and taller ceilings need longer poles. Make sure the frame threads firmly onto the pole without any wobble.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The right roller nap</h3>

          <p>
            Smooth or lightly textured ceilings typically take a 3/8 to 1/2 inch nap, and heavier textures need 3/4 inch or more. Woven microfiber and good synthetic covers hold more paint and release it evenly, which reduces spatter and lap marks. Cheap covers shed fibers and spatter, and both show clearly on a white ceiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A brush for cutting in and a safe ladder</h3>

          <p>
            Cut in with a quality 2 to 2.5 inch angled sash brush. A small stepladder rated for your weight plus tools and paint is safer than a chair; set it on level ground and never stand on the top step. Keep a damp rag within reach to catch drips straight away.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the room and yourself</h3>

          <p>
            Move furniture to the middle of the room or out of it, lay canvas drop cloths that will not slide, and cover furniture with plastic. Take down or tape over light fixtures, smoke detector covers and ceiling fan canopies. Wear safety glasses or goggles and a cap, because even low-spatter paint drips now and then.
          </p>

