        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Do You Paint a Ceiling Without Roller Marks?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cut in one wall at a time, roll small sections back into wet paint, never stop in the middle of the ceiling, and apply two thin coats rather than one heavy one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut in one wall at a time</h3>

          <p>
            Brush a 2 to 3 inch band where the ceiling meets the walls and around fixtures. To avoid lap marks between that band and the rolled field, cut in a single wall and roll that area while the band is still wet, instead of cutting in the whole room first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Roll in 4-by-4-foot sections</h3>

          <p>
            Load the roller evenly from a tray or bucket grid and work in sections about 4 by 4 feet. Start a few inches from the last wet edge and roll back into it, spreading paint in a W or zigzag and then filling in with parallel strokes. Finish each section with light, straight passes in one direction for an even texture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep a wet edge to the end</h3>

          <p>
            Work across the room's shorter dimension so each new section overlaps paint that is still wet. Do not take a break in the middle of a ceiling; finish the whole thing in one session. If you have to stop, end at a natural break such as a beam or a corner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Final strokes and the second coat</h3>

          <p>
            Many painters lay the final strokes parallel to the main window light, which makes leftover roller texture harder to see. Two thin coats beat one heavy coat. Wait the label recoat time, typically a few hours, then roll the second coat perpendicular to the first for more even coverage. Do not go back over areas that have begun to dry; that is what causes flashing and lap marks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Ceiling Paint Is Best for Each Room?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ultra-flat premium paint for bright living spaces, a good flat for bedrooms, mildew-resistant paint for bathrooms, and a primer first on new or stained ceilings. Here is the match room by room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Living rooms, bedrooms and hallways</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Living rooms and bright spaces</strong> need an ultra-flat premium paint such as Benjamin Moore Waterborne Ceiling Paint or Sherwin-Williams Eminence, because these rooms get the most raking light and it shows every flaw in a lesser paint. <strong>Bedrooms and hallways</strong> usually do fine with a good flat paint like BEHR Premium Plus, especially when the ceiling is in good shape.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bathrooms, laundry rooms and kitchens</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Bathrooms and laundry rooms</strong> call for a mildew-resistant paint such as Zinsser Perma-White in satin or eggshell, plus an exhaust fan that works and vents outside. <strong>Kitchens</strong> see grease and steam, which favors a matte or eggshell finish you can wipe down; clean thoroughly before painting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">New drywall, stained and textured ceilings</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>New drywall</strong> gets a PVA drywall primer, then two coats of ceiling paint. <strong>Stained ceilings</strong> need the stains sealed with a shellac or oil-based stain-blocking primer before any paint goes on. <strong>Popcorn or textured ceilings</strong> in older homes should be tested for asbestos first, then rolled with a thick nap or sprayed, using light pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Porch ceilings, basements and garages</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Porch ceilings and exterior soffits</strong> take exterior paint, not interior ceiling paint, because they face humidity and temperature swings; our guide to the <a href="/best-porch-paint/">best porch paint</a> covers porch ceilings, including the traditional haint blue. <strong>Basements and garages</strong> are fine with budget ceiling paints, and the same rooms often benefit from new floor paint; see our pick of the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> for that job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much Ceiling Paint Do You Need, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most ceiling paints list about 350 to 400 square feet per gallon on smooth, primed surfaces, so one gallon usually covers a 12 by 14 foot bedroom ceiling twice. Coverage drops on textured ceilings and new drywall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to estimate gallons</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply the room's length by its width to get the ceiling area. A 12 by 14 foot bedroom ceiling is 168 square feet, which one gallon usually covers in two coats. A 20 by 20 foot living room needs about two gallons for two coats.
          </p>

