        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Ceiling Paint
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over a water stain without sealing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water stains bleed through ordinary ceiling paint, often within hours. Fix the leak, let the area dry, and seal the stain with a shellac or oil-based stain-blocking primer before painting. A ceiling paint labeled stain-resistant may cope with minor marks, but it does not replace primer on real water or smoke damage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking a paint with too much sheen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even slight sheen picks out roller marks, patches and seams once side light hits the ceiling. Most ceilings look best in flat or ultra-flat paint. Save satin or eggshell for rooms where you need to wipe the surface, such as bathrooms and kitchens, and expect it to show more flaws than flat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting in the whole room before rolling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When the brushed band along the walls dries before you roll beside it, brushed and rolled areas can look different, leaving a picture-frame outline around the ceiling. Cut in one wall at a time and roll that stretch while the band is still wet, so the finish stays uniform right to the edges.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping halfway across the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A break mid-ceiling lets the wet edge dry, and a visible lap line appears where you start again. Plan to finish each ceiling in one session; if you must stop, do it at a natural break such as a corner or beam. Working across the room's shorter dimension helps keep the edge wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping primer on new drywall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh drywall and joint compound absorb paint unevenly, so seams and patches flash through the topcoat. A PVA drywall primer seals the surface and lets ceiling paint go on evenly. Leave it out and you often need extra coats of finish paint, and the seams may still show in raking light.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ceiling paint really different from wall paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Ceiling paint is usually thicker to cut drips and spatter, flatter to hide flaws in raking light, and sometimes slower to set so you can keep a wet edge. Wall paint is made to be more washable and comes in more sheens. Using the right product on each surface makes the job easier and the finish better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a ceiling need two coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most ceilings look better with two, especially when you are changing color, painting new drywall or covering patches. A premium high-hide ceiling paint over a similar white in good condition may cover in one. Two thin coats usually give a more uniform finish than one thick coat and do more to hide roller marks and flashing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes roller marks on a ceiling, and how do I avoid them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roller marks usually come from letting the wet edge dry, pressing too hard, running the roller too dry or using a cheap cover. Keep a wet edge, work in small sections, load the roller evenly, use a quality cover with the right nap and finish with light parallel strokes. An ultra-flat ceiling paint also helps hide marks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When does a ceiling need primer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a PVA primer on new drywall, a stain-blocking primer on stains and a spot-prime on patches. A previously painted ceiling in good condition usually needs only cleaning first. When you are going from a dark color to white or covering smoke damage, primer improves coverage and stops bleed-through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will ceiling paint work on walls too?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, but it is a poor fit for most rooms. Flat ceiling paint is not made to be scrubbed, so hands, furniture and cleaning mark it easily. It can work in low-traffic spaces such as closets or guest rooms, but an eggshell or matte wall paint holds up better on walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which paint is best for a bathroom ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mildew-resistant paint such as Zinsser Perma-White in satin or eggshell is a strong choice, because it resists mildew growth and wipes clean. Check that the exhaust fan works and vents outside, and clean off existing mildew thoroughly before you paint. Paint by itself will not solve a ventilation problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I paint a popcorn ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the home was built before the 1980s, have the texture tested for asbestos first. If it is safe and intact, use a thick-nap roller with light pressure, or spray it with a paint sprayer, so the texture does not loosen. Avoid overworking the surface, and buy more paint than for a smooth ceiling, since texture absorbs more.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

